<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="wrap header-inner">
      <a class="brand" href="#top" aria-label="Our Team home">Our Team<span aria-hidden="true">.</span></a>
      <div class="header-actions">
        <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
          <a href="#team">The team</a>
          <a href="#future-of-se">The future</a>
        </nav>
        <button class="theme-toggle" type="button" id="theme-toggle" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Switch to night mode">
          <span aria-hidden="true" id="theme-icon">☾</span><span id="theme-label">Night mode</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>
  <main id="top">
    <div class="wrap hero">
      <span class="eyebrow">People, perspectives &amp; software</span>
      <h1>Engineering,<br>in our own words.</h1>
      <p>Meet five computer science and engineering students thinking critically about what AI means for the craft of building software—and what comes next.</p>
    </div>
    <section class="team-section" id="team" aria-labelledby="team-heading">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <h2 id="team-heading">The team</h2>
          <p>Different interests and outlooks, with one shared curiosity about how technology changes the way we build.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="member-grid">
          <article class="member-card">
            <img src="images/annie.jpg" alt="Photo of Annie" width="128" height="128" loading="lazy">
            <h3>Annie</h3>
            <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Engineering, 5th Semester from October 2026</p>
            <p class="bio">Annie is pursuing an M.Sc. in Computer Engineering and enjoys travelling and cooking. Born in Dubai, she has a birth certificate in Arabic. She sees AI helping software developers work faster by writing code, debugging errors and automating repetitive tasks. She is also wary of how much the future may depend on AI, and believes knowing how to check that generated code is correct and valid is what makes someone a real software engineer.</p>
            <p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Annie was born in Dubai, UAE, and her birth certificate is in Arabic.</em></p>
            <span class="take-label">Hot take on AI</span>
            <blockquote class="hot-take">AI can generate code in seconds, but in the end to check if that code is correct and valid, is what makes someone a real software engineer.</blockquote>
          </article>
          <article class="member-card">
            <img src="images/avishkar.jpeg" alt="Photo of Avishkar" width="128" height="128" loading="lazy">
            <h3>Avishkar</h3>
            <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Engineering, 3rd Semester from October 2026</p>
            <p class="bio">Avishkar is studying for an M.Sc. in Computer Engineering. He enjoys stock market investing and playing 70s–80s band songs on guitar. He has a personal record of 3,000 jump ropes in under 45 minutes. Today, he sees AI as a filter for engineers: strong foundations in system design, security and scaling help people use it well. He expects engineering work to shift toward designing systems and coordinating AI agents, while remaining cautious about near-term growth because of energy demands.</p>
            <p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Avishkar has a personal record of 3,000 jump ropes in under 45 minutes.</em></p>
            <span class="take-label">Hot take on AI</span>
            <blockquote class="hot-take">I don't think AI would scale a lot, atleast not in the short-medium term future. The projected energy demands for AI is way more than what experts think we can produce.</blockquote>
          </article>
          <article class="member-card">
            <img src="images/parastoo.jpeg" alt="Photo of Parastoo" width="128" height="128" loading="lazy">
            <h3>Parastoo</h3>
            <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science student, University of Paderborn, Germany (2026–Present)</p>
            <p class="bio">Parastoo is an M.Sc. Computer Science student at the University of Paderborn, Germany. Her interests include AI, software engineering, data-driven and distributed systems, and real-world technology problems. She says her brain runs at 1.5× speed: she can handle five or more tasks while listening to a podcast at the same speed. She sees AI making coding faster and more accessible, while making fundamentals such as algorithms, architecture, security and problem-solving even more important.</p>
            <p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Parastoo can handle five or more tasks while listening to a podcast at 1.5× speed.</em></p>
            <span class="take-label">Hot take on AI</span>
            <blockquote class="hot-take">AI can generate code, but generating code is not the same as engineering a reliable product. I believe the real challenge is not just making AI more powerful, but making its output trustworthy, secure, efficient, and useful in real-world systems.</blockquote>
          </article>
          <article class="member-card">
            <img src="images/parsa.jpg" alt="Photo of Parsa" width="128" height="128" loading="lazy">
            <h3>Parsa</h3>
            <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Engineering, 2nd Semester from April 2026</p>
            <p class="bio">Parsa is pursuing an M.Sc. in Computer Engineering. He is interested in gaming PC hardware and American politics, and still has not gotten his driver's license. He finds AI very impressive but thinks there may be a bubble. Looking five to ten years ahead, he expects software engineering to be markedly different, with completely different types of jobs and positions. He also imagines that people will care less about AI-generated texts and images, while artists adapt.</p>
            <p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Parsa still hasn't gotten his driver's license.</em></p>
            <span class="take-label">Hot take on AI</span>
            <blockquote class="hot-take">No one will care about AI generated texts or images 10 years from now and artists will adapt.</blockquote>
          </article>
          <article class="member-card">
            <img src="images/sophika.jpg" alt="Photo of Sophika" width="128" height="128" loading="lazy">
            <h3>Sophika</h3>
            <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Engineering, 5th Semester from October 2026</p>
            <p class="bio">Sophika is studying for an M.Sc. in Computer Engineering and enjoys listening to music and reading. She can drive a five-gear motorcycle. She believes AI-generated code is not always reliable, so human understanding, testing and verification remain essential. In the years ahead, she expects software engineers to focus on creative work while AI takes on repetitive coding and automation. She is excited by what AI can build, but mindful of the harm it could cause in the wrong hands.</p>
            <p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Sophika can drive a five-gear motorcycle.</em></p>
            <span class="take-label">Hot take on AI</span>
            <blockquote class="hot-take">AI has the power to build incredible things, but in the wrong hands, it could destroy more than it creates.</blockquote>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="future-section" id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="future-panel">
          <span class="eyebrow">Looking ahead</span>
          <h2 id="future-heading">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
          <p>Across the team, a recurring possibility is that engineers will spend less time writing every line themselves and more time designing systems, setting requirements, making technical decisions and coordinating AI-powered tools. Strong foundations in architecture, security, scaling and problem-solving will matter as much as ever.</p>
          <p>The team is not unanimous about how quickly AI will advance or what its lasting impact will be. Some see a powerful shift in how software is built; others question its scalability or expect today's AI-generated media to matter less over time. A common thread is that generated code still needs human judgment: it must be tested, understood and made reliable, secure and useful in the real world.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <footer>
    <div class="wrap"><p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. 2026 · Our Team</p></div>
  </footer>
  <script>
    (() => {
      const root = document.documentElement;
      const button = document.getElementById('theme-toggle');
      const label = document.getElementById('theme-label');
      const icon = document.getElementById('theme-icon');
      let saved = null;
      try { saved = localStorage.getItem('team-theme'); } catch (_) {}
      const initial = saved === 'dark' || saved === 'light' ? saved : (matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches ? 'dark' : 'light');
      function setTheme(theme) {
        root.dataset.theme = theme;
        const dark = theme === 'dark';
        button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(dark));
        button.setAttribute('aria-label', dark ? 'Switch to day mode' : 'Switch to night mode');
        label.textContent = dark ? 'Day mode' : 'Night mode';
        icon.textContent = dark ? '☀' : '☾';
      }
      setTheme(initial);
      button.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const next = root.dataset.theme === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark';
        setTheme(next);
        try { localStorage.setItem('team-theme', next); } catch (_) {}
      });
    })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>"}

