<article class="member-card"><img src="images/parastoo.jpeg" alt="Photo of Parastoo" width="128" height="128"><h3>Parastoo</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · University of Paderborn, Germany (2026–present)</p><p class="bio">Parastoo studies for an M.Sc. in Computer Science at the University of Paderborn, Germany. Her interests include AI, software engineering, data-driven and distributed systems, and technology that solves real-world problems. Her brain apparently runs at 1.5× speed: she can handle five or more tasks while listening to a podcast at the same speed. She believes AI can make coding faster and more accessible, while engineering fundamentals remain essential to judge its output and build reliable, secure systems.</p><p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Parastoo can handle 5+ tasks while listening to a podcast at 1.5× speed.</em></p><div class="take"><strong>Her hot take on AI</strong><p>AI can generate code, but generating code is not the same as engineering a reliable product. I believe the real challenge is not just making AI more powerful, but making its output trustworthy, secure, efficient, and useful in real-world systems.</p></div></article>
<article class="member-card"><img src="images/parsa.jpg" alt="Photo of Parsa" width="128" height="128"><h3>Parsa</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Engineering · 2nd semester (from April 2026)</p><p class="bio">Parsa is pursuing an M.Sc. in Computer Engineering, entering his 2nd semester from April 2026. He is interested in gaming PC hardware and American politics. He still has not gotten his driver's license. He finds AI very impressive, but suspects there is a bubble around it. Looking five to ten years ahead, he expects software engineering to change markedly, with completely different types of jobs and positions emerging.</p><p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Parsa still hasn't gotten his driver's license.</em></p><div class="take"><strong>His hot take on AI</strong><p>No one will care about AI generated texts or images 10 years from now and artists will adapt.</p></div></article>
<article class="member-card"><img src="images/sophika.jpg" alt="Photo of Sophika" width="128" height="128"><h3>Sophika</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Engineering · 5th semester (from October 2026)</p><p class="bio">Sophika is pursuing an M.Sc. in Computer Engineering, entering her 5th semester from October 2026. She enjoys listening to music and reading. She can drive a five-gear motorcycle. Sophika believes AI-generated code is not always reliable, so human understanding, testing, and verification remain essential. In the future, she expects software engineers to bring creativity while AI takes on repetitive coding and automation tasks.</p><p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Sophika can drive a 5-gear motorcycle.</em></p><div class="take"><strong>Her hot take on AI</strong><p>AI has the power to build incredible things, but in the wrong hands, it could destroy more than it creates.</p></div></article>
</div></section>
<section id="future-of-se" class="future" aria-labelledby="future-title"><div class="wrap"><div class="future-intro"><div class="eyebrow">Our shared perspective</div><h2 id="future-title">The Future of Software Engineering</h2><p>We expect the work to change, not disappear. As AI helps generate code and automate routine tasks, engineers will spend more time shaping systems, making decisions, and ensuring that what gets built is worth trusting.</p></div><div class="future-points"><div class="point"><h3>Design the whole system</h3><p>Architecture, requirements, scaling, and security will matter as much as implementation. Engineers will guide tools and agents toward a coherent solution.</p></div><div class="point"><h3>Keep the human judgment</h3><p>Generated output still needs to be understood, tested, and verified. Reliability and real-world usefulness are engineering responsibilities, not automatic outcomes.</p></div><div class="point"><h3>Make room for creativity</h3><p>AI can take on repetitive work, leaving people more space for creative problem-solving—while society adapts to new roles and to the limits of the technology.</p></div></div></div></section></main>
