<body>
<header><a class="brand" href="#top">GROUP 18 · SOFTWARE ENGINEERING</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to dark theme" aria-pressed="false">🌙 <span>Night mode</span></button></header>
<main id="top">
<section class="wrap hero" aria-labelledby="page-heading"><div class="eyebrow">People behind the perspective</div><h1 id="page-heading">Software engineering, in our own words.</h1><p>Five computer science and engineering students share what they care about, how they see AI today, and what they think comes next.</p></section>
<section class="wrap" id="team" aria-labelledby="team-heading"><div class="section-heading"><h2 id="team-heading">Meet the team</h2><p>Different interests and instincts, one shared curiosity about building dependable software.</p></div>
<div class="team-grid">
<article class="member-card"><img src="images/annie.jpg" alt="Photo of Annie"><h3>Annie</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Engineering · 5th Semester, from October 2026</p><p class="bio">Annie is studying for an M.Sc. in Computer Engineering and enjoys travelling and cooking. Born in Dubai, UAE, she has a birth certificate written in Arabic. She sees AI as a way to speed software development by helping with code writing, debugging, and repetitive work. She is also concerned that software engineering could be almost dead in five to ten years, and worries about AI taking over the world. For Annie, checking that generated code is correct and valid is what makes someone a real software engineer.</p><p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Annie was born in Dubai, UAE, and her birth certificate is in Arabic.</em></p><div class="hot-take">🔥 Checking AI-generated code is what makes someone a real software engineer.</div></article>
<article class="member-card"><img src="images/avishkar.jpeg" alt="Photo of Avishkar"><h3>Avishkar</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Engineering · 3rd Semester, from October 2026</p><p class="bio">Avishkar is pursuing an M.Sc. in Computer Engineering, with interests in stock market investing and playing 70s–80s band songs on guitar. He has a personal record of 3,000 jump ropes in under 45 minutes. He views AI as a filter that distinguishes engineers with strong fundamentals in system design, security, and scaling. Looking ahead, he expects engineers to spend more time designing systems and coordinating AI agents than writing code. He is skeptical that AI will scale rapidly in the short to medium term, partly because of its projected energy demands.</p><p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Avishkar's personal record is 3,000 jump ropes in under 45 minutes.</em></p><div class="hot-take">🔥 AI may not scale quickly; its projected energy demands are enormous.</div></article>
<article class="member-card"><img src="images/parastoo.jpeg" alt="Photo of Parastoo"><h3>Parastoo</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · University of Paderborn, Germany · 2026–Present</p><p class="bio">Parastoo studies Computer Science at the University of Paderborn, Germany. Her interests include artificial intelligence, software engineering, data-driven and distributed systems, and applying technology to real-world problems. Her brain apparently runs at 1.5x speed: she can handle more than five tasks while listening to a podcast at the same speed. She believes AI makes coding faster and more accessible, while making engineering fundamentals more important. In her view, trustworthy, secure, efficient, and useful output—not simply more powerful code generation—is the real challenge.</p><p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Parastoo can handle 5+ tasks while listening to a podcast at 1.5x speed.</em></p><div class="hot-take">🔥 Generating code is not the same as engineering a reliable product.</div></article>
<article class="member-card"><img src="images/parsa.jpg" alt="Photo of Parsa"><h3>Parsa</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Engineering · 2nd Semester, from April 2026</p><p class="bio">Parsa is studying for an M.Sc. in Computer Engineering. He is interested in gaming PC hardware and American politics, and still has not gotten his driver's license. He finds AI very impressive, though he thinks there may be a bubble. Looking five to ten years ahead, Parsa expects software engineering to be markedly different from today, with completely different kinds of jobs and positions. He also believes that AI-generated texts and images will matter less to people in ten years, and that artists will adapt.</p><p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Parsa still hasn't gotten his driver's license.</em></p><div class="hot-take">🔥 In ten years, no one will care about AI-generated texts or images.</div></article>
<article class="member-card"><img src="images/sophika.jpg" alt="Photo of Sophika"><h3>Sophika</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Engineering · 5th Semester, from October 2026</p><p class="bio">Sophika is pursuing an M.Sc. in Computer Engineering and enjoys listening to music and reading. She can drive a five-gear motorcycle. She thinks AI-generated code is not always reliable, so human understanding, testing, and verification remain essential. In the future, she sees software engineers as the creative ones, with AI taking on repetitive coding and automated tasks. Her perspective highlights both the potential to build incredible things and the danger of putting powerful tools in the wrong hands.</p><p class="fact"><em>Fun fact: Sophika can drive a 5-gear motorcycle.</em></p><div class="hot-take">🔥 In the wrong hands, AI could destroy more than it creates.</div></article>
</div></section>
<section class="wrap future" id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-heading"><div class="future-panel"><div class="eyebrow">Our shared perspective</div><h2 id="future-heading">The Future of Software Engineering</h2><p>We see AI making software development faster, more accessible, and increasingly automated—but not removing the need for human engineering. Across the team, a strong foundation in architecture, security, systems thinking, and problem-solving matters for directing AI and judging its output. Engineers may shift toward designing systems, making technical decisions, and coordinating AI-powered tools, while people remain responsible for reliability, testing, and useful outcomes.</p><div class="future-points"><div class="point"><strong>Build the foundations</strong><span>Fundamentals make it possible to evaluate generated code and make sound technical choices.</span></div><div class="point"><strong>Design the bigger picture</strong><span>System architecture, requirements, security, and coordination grow in importance.</span></div><div class="point"><strong>Keep people accountable</strong><span>Testing and verification help ensure software is reliable and genuinely useful.</span></div></div></div></section>
</main>
<footer>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. · 2026 · Group 18</footer>
